  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Compact Operators [S+N]\n\nA bounded linear $T : X \\to Y$ between Banach spaces is **compact** if $T$ takes bounded sets to **relatively compact** (precompact) sets — that is, $\\overline{T(B)}$ is compact for every bounded $B$.\n\nEquivalent characterisation: every bounded sequence $(x_n)$ in $X$ has a subsequence with $T x_{n_k}$ convergent in $Y$.\n\nIn finite dimensions, every bounded operator is compact (Heine-Borel). In infinite dimensions, compactness is a *strict* condition — the identity $I : \\ell^2 \\to \\ell^2$ is bounded but **not** compact (orthonormal sequences have no convergent subsequence).\n\nCompact operators behave almost like matrices: they have eigenvalues, can be diagonalised (in Hilbert space, when self-adjoint), and the Fredholm alternative holds. They're the \"tame\" infinite-dimensional operators.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Examples and non-examples\n\n**Compact**:\n- Finite-rank operators ($T$ with finite-dimensional range).\n- Hilbert-Schmidt integral operators on $L^2$ with kernel in $L^2$.\n- The Volterra operator $V f(x) = \\int_0^x f$ on $L^2([0, 1])$.\n- Limits (in operator norm) of compact operators.\n\n**Not compact**:\n- The identity $I$ on any infinite-dimensional Banach space.\n- Multiplication by a non-zero $\\varphi \\in L^\\infty$ on $L^2$ (unless $\\varphi$ vanishes outside a finite-measure set — then it's compact iff $\\varphi$ is \"small enough\").\n- The shift operator on $\\ell^2$.\n\n## Approximation by finite-rank operators\n\nIn a Hilbert space, compact operators are **exactly** the operator-norm limits of finite-rank operators. This makes them computationally accessible: truncate to the leading $N$ singular vectors and the error decays as $N \\to \\infty$.\n\nFor a Hilbert-Schmidt operator $T$ on $L^2$ with kernel $K(x, y)$, expand $K$ in an orthonormal basis $\\{e_n\\}$:\n\n$$K(x, y) = \\sum_{m, n} c_{mn} e_m(x) \\overline{e_n(y)}, \\qquad T f = \\sum_{m, n} c_{mn} \\langle f, e_n \\rangle e_m.$$\n\nThe truncation $T_N$ to indices $m, n \\le N$ is finite-rank, and $\\|T - T_N\\| \\to 0$ as $N \\to \\infty$ by Parseval.\n\n## Spectral picture: Riesz-Schauder\n\nFor a compact $T$ on a Banach space:\n\n1. The spectrum $\\sigma(T) \\setminus \\{0\\}$ consists of **eigenvalues**, each with **finite-dimensional eigenspace**.\n2. The only possible accumulation point of $\\sigma(T)$ is $0$.\n3. If $\\dim X = \\infty$, then $0 \\in \\sigma(T)$ (since $I - T$ being invertible would mean $T$ is identity-minus-compact, perturbing $I$, but $I$ itself isn't compact).\n\nSo the spectrum looks like a discrete set of eigenvalues (each finite multiplicity) plus possibly $0$.\n\nThis is *almost* the matrix picture, with the caveat that there might be an accumulation at $0$ — but the *non-zero* part of the spectrum behaves exactly like matrix eigenvalues.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Demonstrate with a finite-rank approximation of a compact operator on ℓ². */\n/* Diagonal operator T(e_n) = (1/n) e_n is compact (eigenvalues 1, 1/2, 1/3, ... → 0). */\ndiag_evals : makelist(1.0/n, n, 1, 20)$\nprint(\"Eigenvalues of compact diagonal operator T(e_n) = (1/n) e_n:\")$\nprint(\"first 10:\", makelist(diag_evals[k], k, 1, 10))$\nprint()$\nprint(\"Accumulate at 0; truncated 10x10 has 'leading' part {1, 1/2, ..., 1/10}.\")$\nax_draw2d(\n  marker_size=8, color=\"blue\", name=\"eigenvalues 1/n\",\n  points(makelist(n, n, 1, 20), diag_evals),\n  color=\"black\", dash=\"dash\", explicit(0, t, 0, 20),\n  title=\"Spectrum of compact operator T(e_n) = (1/n)e_n — accumulates at 0\",\n  xlabel=\"n\", ylabel=\"λ_n\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Eigenvalues of compact diagonal operator T(e_n) = (1/n) e_n:\nfirst 10:\n         [1.0,0.5,0.3333333333333333,0.25,0.2,0.16666666666666666,\n          0.14285714285714285,0.125,0.1111111111111111,0.1]\nAccumulate at 0; truncated 10x10 has 'leading' part {1, 1/2, ..., 1/10}."
          ]
