    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Fredholm alternative\n\nFor compact $T$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$:\n\n$$\\lambda I - T \\text{ is injective } \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; \\lambda I - T \\text{ is surjective } \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; \\lambda I - T \\text{ is invertible}.$$\n\nIn words: for compact perturbations of the identity, **injectivity = surjectivity = bijectivity**. This generalises a basic fact about square matrices to infinite dimensions.\n\nApplication: integral equations of the form $\\lambda f(x) - \\int K(x, y) f(y)\\,dy = g(x)$. Either the homogeneous equation ($g = 0$) has only the trivial solution (then the inhomogeneous has unique solution for every $g$), or the homogeneous has non-trivial solutions (and the inhomogeneous is solvable iff $g$ is orthogonal to the kernel of the adjoint).\n\n## Self-adjoint compact operators on Hilbert space\n\nWhen the compact operator is also self-adjoint ($T = T^*$), we get the cleanest result:\n\n**Spectral theorem.** $T$ has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors with real eigenvalues $\\lambda_n \\to 0$, and\n\n$$T = \\sum_n \\lambda_n \\,P_n$$\n\nwhere $P_n$ is orthogonal projection onto the eigenspace of $\\lambda_n$.\n\nThis is matrix diagonalisation, lifted to infinite dimensions. The price: only countably many non-zero eigenvalues, accumulating at zero.\n\n## Why compactness matters in practice\n\nCompact operators are where you can:\n\n- **Solve** integral equations numerically with provable convergence (Galerkin methods).\n- **Diagonalise** elliptic-PDE-style operators (after inverting them — the inverse of an unbounded elliptic operator is often compact).\n- **Approximate** infinite-dimensional problems with finite-dimensional ones (truncated singular value decompositions, principal component analysis).\n- **Represent** the smoothing effect of physical operators (heat propagation, low-pass filters).\n\n## Where this leaves us\n\nCompact operators bridge finite-dimensional intuition and the infinite-dimensional generality of bounded operators. They have eigenvalues like matrices, but with the accumulation-at-zero subtlety. They can be approximated by matrices with quantifiable error.\n\nThe next notebook builds on this with the **spectral theorem for self-adjoint operators** in full generality — including unbounded ones via the spectral measure formulation.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
